1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,885
The history of the role of infinity in math is

2
00:00:02,985 --> 00:00:06,195
very interesting, and will have its own video soon.

3
00:00:06,266 --> 00:00:10,151
For now just note, that due to various paradoxes, infinity was only

4
00:00:10,251 --> 00:00:14,670
accepted theoretically. Meaning that the sentence for all y, there exists an

5
00:00:14,770 --> 00:00:17,347
x such that x is greater than y. was accepted

6
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:21,557
But it was not accepted that there exists an x for all y, such that

7
00:00:21,657 --> 00:00:25,815
x is greater than y, since such an x would be a truly infinite set.

8
00:00:25,866 --> 00:00:28,151
It took this man right here, Georg

9
00:00:28,251 --> 00:00:30,886
Cantor to develop a theory of infinity.

10
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,478
The first obstacle we encounter is the seeming paradox we

11
00:00:34,578 --> 00:00:37,824
encounter when trying to compare the sizes of infinite sets.

12
00:00:37,924 --> 00:00:40,612
For example, if we compare the size of the natural

13
00:00:40,712 --> 00:00:42,898
numbers with the set of square numbers...

14
00:00:42,933 --> 00:00:45,664
on the one hand, the set of natural numbers seems

15
00:00:45,764 --> 00:00:48,841
way larger, since most numbers are not perfect squares.

16
00:00:48,933 --> 00:00:52,622
And in fact the difference in size increases with larger numbers.

17
00:00:52,722 --> 00:00:55,886
The squares make up 10 percent of numbers less than 100.

18
00:00:55,933 --> 00:00:58,419
1 per cent of numbers less than 10K

19
00:00:58,466 --> 00:01:02,389
and 0.1 percent of numbers less than a million and so on.

20
00:01:03,466 --> 00:01:07,765
But on the other hand, every natural number has exactly one square. So, at

21
00:01:07,865 --> 00:01:11,569
infinity, we have to admit that the two sets have the same size.

22
00:01:11,666 --> 00:01:13,745
Now, to most of you, this just means that

23
00:01:13,845 --> 00:01:16,348
sets are either finite or infinite, and once they

24
00:01:16,448 --> 00:01:19,377
are infinite, their size cannot be increased or compared.

25
00:01:20,466 --> 00:01:24,244
But to Cantor, the defining characteristic of size, was whether the

26
00:01:24,344 --> 00:01:27,832
two sets could be put into correspondence with each other. So,

27
00:01:27,932 --> 00:01:30,841
to Cantor, these two do literally have the same size

28
00:01:30,933 --> 00:01:34,191
and Cantor's friend Dedekind defined a set to be infinite

29
00:01:34,291 --> 00:01:36,370
if and only if it could be put into 1

30
00:01:36,470 --> 00:01:39,375
to 1 correspondence with a proper subset of itself.

31
00:01:39,466 --> 00:01:41,586
So, the existence of this 1-1 mapping just

32
00:01:41,686 --> 00:01:43,911
shows that the natural numbers are infinite.

33
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,512
Cantor then showed how to put the rational numbers

34
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,352
and even the algebraic numbers (which include certain

35
00:01:49,452 --> 00:01:51,882
irrational numbers like the square root of two)

36
00:01:51,982 --> 00:01:54,519
into 1-1 correspondence with the natural numbers.

37
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:57,816
Some would say that set theory was born when Cantor showed that

38
00:01:57,916 --> 00:02:01,397
this pattern breaks once we hit the Real numbers. Meaning that there

39
00:02:01,497 --> 00:02:04,766
is no way to put the naturals and reals into 1-1 correspondence.

40
00:02:04,866 --> 00:02:08,083
And therefore the set of real numbers are a larger infinite set

41
00:02:08,133 --> 00:02:09,496
We sketch this proof now.

